<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Ifat</h1><p class="st-meta">1285 CE – 1415 CE (approx.) · capital Walale</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Muslim power of medieval Ethiopia, ruled by the Walasma dynasty and the main rival of the Solomonic emperors for over a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1285 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-walasma">Umar Walasma</a>, of a family claiming Arab descent, conquered the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-shewa">Sultanate of Shewa</a> and founded a new state in the eastern highlands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Shewa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> controlled the trade route from the interior to the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> and soon drew other Muslim principalities, such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-dawaro">Dawaro</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/fatagar-sultanate">Fatagar</a>, into its orbit.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a> was a prosperous Muslim state of towns, farms and caravans, exporting slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, gold and horses through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. The Arab writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a> described it as the largest of the seven Muslim states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Abyssinia</a>, with a well-ordered court. Its sultans alternated between paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> and rebelling: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabr-ad-din-i">Sabr ad-Din I's</a> revolt was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> in 1332.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Repeated wars ended in disaster when Ethiopian forces killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-ad-din-ii">Sa'ad ad-Din II</a>, traditionally near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, in the early 15th century (c. 1403 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawit-i">Dawit I</a> or c. 1415 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeshaq-i">Yeshaq</a>; the sources differ). His sons fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a>, returned and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> further east, which carried on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walasma</a> line and the struggle against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-ifat.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Ifat" width="320" height="318"><figcaption>Image: Skilla1st · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sultanate_of_Ifat.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1285 CE – 1415 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Walale</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Umar Walasma, Sabr ad-Din I, Sa'ad ad-Din II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Argobba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, <a href="/countries/djibouti">Djibouti</a>, <a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ifat Sultanate, Ifat, Awfat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1350&amp;focus=sultanate-of-ifat" aria-label="Sultanate of Ifat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5774 1857 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Ifat lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6094 2113L6135 204